% 48z-self-hosting-codegen.weft — Stage 8-final: the ARM64 code generator,
% written in C, compiled by kcc itself, and linked back into the compiler.

\chapter{Self-Hosting: The ARM64 Code Generator in C}
\label{ch:self-hosting-codegen}

The code generator of Chapters~\ref{ch:codegen}--\ref{ch:codegen} is the compiler's
back-end spine: it walks the neutral IR and emits ARM64 machine code, byte for
byte the object clang would produce.  Porting it---with the linear-scan register
allocator, spill-slot colouring, the AAPCS64 argument marshaller, the FP
d-register bank, and the per-opcode instruction dispatch---leaves only the IR
builder in assembly.  Because every generated instruction is checked against
clang by \texttt{difftest}, this is the most byte-sensitive port in the whole
self-hosting effort: a single different register assignment or slot colour would
change the machine code and fail the comparison.

The C form is far more compact than the assembly it replaces, because each
mov/bl/bl triple that computed an instruction word and appended it collapses to
one nested call: \texttt{kcc\_code\_emit32(kcc\_enc\_add\_reg(...))}.

\section{State, Constants, Externs}

